ion  sequencing  was uh last year working together uh  with a new jersey hospital and health  care system where they  where they identify that there was an  evidence  of high drug resistance  uh not just for medicine that was seen  in staphylococcus auroras  but when they shifted to prescribing a  different drugs uh  through metoprene supramethoxazole they  found that there might be a resistance  to both drugs so  they sent us isolates and we used next  generation sequencing to identify these  strains  um and indeed what we found that there  was an  evidence for both drug resistance and we  also additionally  found that in two thirds of these  samples mupirosen  resistance was found that means that uh  um that's very alarming because that's  for example an antibiotic that's used on  the skin for decolonization before  surgery  so these pathogens are quickly can be  resistant uh based on the  therapy and prophylaxis that has been  used that's why it's so  important to use precision infectious  disease  therapy that means that we treat exactly  that pathogen that can be detected in  the patient  so and again just to highlight with a  couple of numbers uh  what i said uh it's quite shocking that  currently empirical antibiotics that  means that we  use antibiotics for the patients  based on population based studies  that means that 91 percent we use  empirical antibiotics for the patients  and only using clinical metagenomics and  sequencing that could decrease  uh the unnecessary days of antibiotic  therapy and decrease  the antimicrobial resistance if we if we  use this novel technology  so if you have time right now i  recommend to watch the resistance  fighters it's a great documentary  on anti-microbial resistance and the  global crisis uh  crisis uh we have been facing currently  and  last year the cdc uh has been alerting  and alarming uh this very important case  uh  to work against anti-microbial  resistance so bioshock is also  part of this effort to decrease  antimicrobial resistance and work again  work with anti-macrobio stewardship  so but everybody's companies and  everybody is focusing on  shifted to kobit 19 pandemic and so  today i saved a little time to talk  about that and how next-generation  sequencing this  emerging technology can be used and why  is it  important to use uh during the pandemic  so  i don't want to talk much details about  the virus i'm sure everybody is  monitoring  uh the state this is a  picture from april 16 from john hopkins  um  website where they're showing we are  over two million people  that were diagnosed with coping 19 and  we are reaching 150 000  patients who died from kobe 19  around the world how you can use  sequencing and why is it important  as implication for public health  intervention  genome sequencing in viral outbreaks  is desire desirable to characterize the  infectious agents  determine its evolutionary rate identify  the signature of host adaptation  track transmission route identify  monitor diagnostic targets  and characterize the responses to  vaccines and treatment that's incredibly  important  and also to determine the role of  co-infections when we do comprehensive  metagenomics so this is just an example  how all the collaborators got together  and have been  sequencing what is uh samples  and upload data to  a server where we can analyze of  variants and mutation rates and track  the spread of the virus identify  clusters and transmissions so we can  learn more about the virus spreading  and also like  by countries you can see the colors  differently um  for example where what's the origin  of the virus which was identified  from patient samples so this is a great  website to follow  gis aid um there is a great  collaboration and it's incredible how  globally all the researchers came  together to track the virus using  sequencing  also i would like to highlight the  effort of the center for  disease control and prevention there are  currently  around 37 ma